== 7. adversarial cases of our own design ==
  [PASS] A1 re-encode the protected bstr length head (58cd -> 5900cd): signature STILL VALID, artifact bytes/sha256/length CHANGE (confirms the vector's F3/A1 note)  -- 332 bytes, sha256 e58c691ecb5d0a1e
  [PASS] A2 a STRICT RFC 8949 s4.2.1 decoder rejects that re-encoding  -- non-minimal length head: argument 205 encoded in 2 bytes at 2
  [PASS] A3 the same re-encoding INSIDE the protected bucket breaks the signature (those bytes are signed)
  [PASS] A4 truncated signature (63 bytes) -> rejected
  [PASS] A5a S+L malleability: rejected when RFC 8032 s5.1.7's 0<=S<L range check is enforced
  [PASS] A5b ... and ACCEPTED without it -- a second valid 64-byte signature over the same statement; verifiers omitting the range check lose signature uniqueness  -- S+L = cb2ef1d5ef03b37e377359b5bba5ebfeb07bb5808ea4edb3bb13ad8c04d5a713
  [PASS] A6 claim confusion: a CWT parser would read the payload as iss/sub/aud/exp with wrong TYPES; the protected content type is what forbids that reading (B1 F2)  -- iss=<32 bytes, want tstr>, sub=0, aud=11, exp='pala-1/v1.0'
  [PASS] A7 tag 98 (COSE_Sign) presented as COSE_Sign1 -> rejected  -- tag 98 is COSE_Sign (multi-signature), not COSE_Sign1
  [PASS] A8 truncated statement -> rejected  -- truncated string: want 64 bytes at 267
  [PASS] A9 trailing byte after the statement -> rejected  -- trailing data: 1 byte(s) after the top-level item
  [PASS] A10 detached payload (nil) + the payload supplied out of band: the SAME signature verifies over a DIFFERENT artifact -- another artifact-identity vs signature-validity split
  [PASS] A11 duplicate map key in the protected bucket: a length-driven decoder accepts it; RFC 8949 s5.6 calls the result invalid, so reject duplicates explicitly  -- our decoder surfaces both keys; a dict-building decoder would silently keep one -- and which one it keeps decides the kid

